1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,055
Lesson 0.5.

2
00:00:02,435 --> 00:00:03,542
Markov chains.

3
00:00:04,650 --> 00:00:08,193
A Gibbs sampler, a pbit chip
or a particle hopping between

4
00:00:08,193 --> 00:00:11,919
wells have something in common:
their next state depends only

5
00:00:11,919 --> 00:00:15,340
on the current state, and on
nothing else from the past.

6
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:17,285
That is a Markov chain.

7
00:00:17,664 --> 00:00:20,894
Markov chains are the
mathematical engine of sampling.

8
00:00:21,274 --> 00:00:24,958
In this lesson we will see how
a chain forgets its starting

9
00:00:24,958 --> 00:00:28,080
point, which distribution
it reaches and how fast.

10
00:00:29,183 --> 00:00:31,955
Imagine a walker on a three-state graph.

11
00:00:32,335 --> 00:00:35,326
At each step it rolls a loaded
die that depends only on

12
00:00:35,326 --> 00:00:38,208
the node it is at, and jumps
according to the result.

13
00:00:38,588 --> 00:00:43,960
The arrows show the transition probabilities:
from state one, for example, it stays

14
00:00:43,960 --> 00:00:48,620
with probability two thirds and moves
to two with probability one third.

15
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,110
If we count how often it visits
each state, the fractions

16
00:00:53,110 --> 00:00:56,787
settle: three tenths, four
tenths and three tenths.

17
00:00:57,167 --> 00:00:59,817
That is the long-run
behavior of the chain.

18
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:03,500
Instead of following
a single walker, let's

19
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:05,990
follow the whole probability distribution.

20
00:01:06,370 --> 00:01:10,867
It is a row vector p_n,
and one step of the chain

21
00:01:10,867 --> 00:01:16,006
multiplies it by the transition
matrix: p_{n+1} = p_n P.

22
00:01:16,386 --> 00:01:19,636
Let's start with all the
probability in state one.

23
00:01:20,016 --> 00:01:24,104
The bars change step by step and
approach the stationary distribution.

24
00:01:24,484 --> 00:01:29,529
If we start in state three, or in any
mixture, we reach the same destination.

25
00:01:29,909 --> 00:01:31,791
The chain forgets where it came from.

26
00:01:33,150 --> 00:01:35,821
The limiting distribution does not change

27
00:01:35,821 --> 00:01:38,361
when one more step is applied: π P = π.

28
00:01:38,741 --> 00:01:43,639
It is a left eigenvector of the
transition matrix, with eigenvalue one.

29
00:01:44,019 --> 00:01:48,444
For an irreducible and aperiodic chain,
that is, one that can go from any

30
00:01:48,444 --> 00:01:52,506
state to any other and does not
oscillate with a fixed period, that

31
00:01:52,506 --> 00:01:57,174
distribution exists, is unique, and the
chain converges to it from any start.

32
00:01:58,283 --> 00:02:01,808
There is a stronger condition than

33
00:02:01,808 --> 00:02:07,199
stationarity: detailed
balance, π_i P_ij = π_j P_ji.

34
00:02:07,579 --> 00:02:11,347
It means that, at equilibrium,
the probability flow from

35
00:02:11,347 --> 00:02:14,644
i to j equals the flow
from j to i on every edge.

36
00:02:15,024 --> 00:02:18,481
In our chain the flows
cancel edge by edge.

37
00:02:18,861 --> 00:02:22,760
Now look at a chain that rotates
around a cycle: its stationary

38
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:26,102
distribution is uniform, but
a net current circulates.

39
00:02:26,482 --> 00:02:29,316
It is stationary, but it does
not satisfy detailed balance.

40
00:02:29,696 --> 00:02:32,948
Chains that satisfy detailed
balance are called reversible,

41
00:02:32,948 --> 00:02:35,979
and they describe systems in
thermodynamic equilibrium.

42
00:02:36,359 --> 00:02:40,863
If detailed balance holds,
stationarity follows automatically.

43
00:02:41,983 --> 00:02:43,729
How fast is the start forgotten?

44
00:02:44,109 --> 00:02:46,765
The answer lies in the other eigenvalues.

45
00:02:47,145 --> 00:02:48,927
The largest is always one.

46
00:02:49,307 --> 00:02:53,253
The second largest in absolute
value, λ₂, controls the

47
00:02:53,253 --> 00:02:57,053
speed: the distance to
stationarity decays as ∣λ₂∣ⁿ.

48
00:02:57,433 --> 00:03:00,687
On a log scale, the
distance falls in a straight

49
00:03:00,687 --> 00:03:03,398
line, with slope equal to the log of λ₂.

50
00:03:03,778 --> 00:03:09,318
The spectral gap is one minus λ₂, and
the mixing time grows as its inverse.

51
00:03:09,698 --> 00:03:14,569
Chains with a small gap take a long
time to forget; in Ising models near

52
00:03:14,569 --> 00:03:19,033
the critical temperature the gap
closes and sampling becomes slow.

53
00:03:20,150 --> 00:03:21,847
Let's check with our chain.

54
00:03:22,227 --> 00:03:26,227
We solve π P = π with the
sum equal to one, and get

55
00:03:26,227 --> 00:03:29,600
three tenths, four
tenths and three tenths.

56
00:03:29,980 --> 00:03:35,151
Let's check detailed balance between states
one and two: three tenths times one third

57
00:03:35,151 --> 00:03:39,531
gives one tenth, and four tenths times
one quarter also gives one tenth.

58
00:03:39,911 --> 00:03:44,349
The eigenvalues of the matrix are
one, two thirds and one sixth.

59
00:03:44,729 --> 00:03:48,413
The spectral gap is one
third, so each step shrinks

60
00:03:48,413 --> 00:03:51,953
the distance to stationarity
to about two thirds.

61
00:03:53,083 --> 00:03:56,499
Markov chains show up
everywhere in the course.

62
00:03:56,879 --> 00:03:59,713
Gibbs and Metropolis are
chains designed so that their

63
00:03:59,713 --> 00:04:02,547
stationary distribution is
the Boltzmann distribution.

64
00:04:02,927 --> 00:04:06,172
A pbit chip is a physical
Markov chain, and its

65
00:04:06,172 --> 00:04:09,486
spectral gap decides how
many sweeps are needed.

66
00:04:09,866 --> 00:04:13,775
In lab one you will build chains
with three and fifty states,

67
00:04:13,775 --> 00:04:17,362
and measure convergence against
the spectral prediction.

68
00:04:18,483 --> 00:04:21,087
In the next lesson we will see that, on a

69
00:04:21,087 --> 00:04:23,753
cycle, the eigenvectors are Fourier waves.

70
00:04:24,133 --> 00:04:27,892
In unit one the stationary distribution
will be the Boltzmann distribution.

71
00:04:28,272 --> 00:04:32,375
The Fokker–Planck equation is the
continuous version of what we saw today.

72
00:04:32,755 --> 00:04:37,553
And in unit four we will measure in
practice how correlated the samples are.

73
00:04:38,683 --> 00:04:42,574
Watch the conventions: if the
distribution is a row vector, it

74
00:04:42,574 --> 00:04:46,528
multiplies from the left and the
rows of the matrix sum to one.

75
00:04:46,908 --> 00:04:50,029
Remember that stationary does
not imply detailed balance.

76
00:04:50,409 --> 00:04:55,415
A periodic chain can have a stationary
distribution and still not converge.

77
00:04:55,795 --> 00:04:59,245
And the speed is decided by
the absolute value of the

78
00:04:59,245 --> 00:05:02,889
second eigenvalue: one near
minus one also slows mixing.

79
00:05:04,017 --> 00:05:09,115
In summary: a Markov chain is
described by its transition

80
00:05:09,115 --> 00:05:13,766
matrix, and distributions
evolve as p_{n+1} = p_n P.

81
00:05:14,146 --> 00:05:17,052
The stationary distribution
satisfies π P = π,

82
00:05:17,052 --> 00:05:19,769
and detailed balance
describes equilibrium.

83
00:05:20,149 --> 00:05:24,216
The spectral gap sets how fast
the chain forgets its start.

84
00:05:25,317 --> 00:05:29,887
To practice: prove that detailed
balance implies stationarity.

85
00:05:30,267 --> 00:05:34,698
Find the stationary distribution of a
two-state chain and its second eigenvalue.

86
00:05:35,078 --> 00:05:39,428
Compute how many steps are needed to
reach a distance of one millionth.

87
00:05:39,808 --> 00:05:42,078
And build a chain with
a uniform stationary

88
00:05:42,078 --> 00:05:44,401
distribution that
violates detailed balance.
